* Fail2Ban
** Goal
*** Rate-limit authentication abuse
*** Read logs, write firewall rules
*** Not a replacement for strong auth
** Architecture
*** fail2ban-client (config reader)
*** UNIX socket, pickle protocol
*** fail2ban-server (one process)
*** Jail = filter + actions
*** SQLite database
*** Observer thread
** Filter
*** Backends: polling, pyinotify, systemd
*** failregex with <HOST> tag
*** ignoreregex, ignoreip, ignorecommand
*** DateDetector, findtime window
*** FailTicket per failure
** Actions
*** BanManager holds ban tickets
*** actionstart / check / ban / unban
*** CommandAction runs /bin/sh
*** iptables, nftables, pf, hostsdeny
*** Reporting: mail, abuseipdb, cloudflare
** Privilege model
*** Server runs as root
*** No privilege separation in code
*** umask 0077, SocketMode 0600
*** Socket access equals root
** Risks
*** Log injection picks the banned IP
*** Greedy regex before <HOST>
*** Regex cost on attacker-controlled lines
*** Shell metacharacters in tags
*** Self-lockout and DNS trust
@endmindmap
